// fixtures.test.maxon — the shared half of `maxon test`'s own gate: where the fixture corpus is, how one
// case is run, and the two things asked of the corpus itself. The per-case `test` declarations are the
// files beside this one, named for the cases they cover.
//
// ⭐⭐ **WHAT THIS PROJECT PROVES: the REPORT is the command's product, to the byte.** Each case file
// spawns this checkout's `maxon-shv2` at one small project kept under `../test-fixtures/`, and compares
// the stdout and the exit code against the two files that project keeps beside its sources. A runner
// whose verdict a reader cannot trust to the byte is a runner nobody reads.
//
// ⭐⭐ **AND THE CIRCULARITY IS CLOSED BY THE BOOTSTRAP, WHICH IS WHY IT IS NOT ONE.** These tests
// are meant to be run BOTH ways, and CI must do both:
//
//     maxon-shv2/.maxon/maxon-shv2 test tests/test-command
//     bin/maxon                    test tests/test-command
//
// Both must pass and both must report the SAME NUMBER OF TESTS. The bootstrap's `maxon test` is an
// independent implementation of the command under test, so if shv2's runner ever lies — reports green
// having run nothing, the one failure a self-hosted gate structurally cannot catch — the bootstrap's run
// still reports honestly. Note what the two runs share and do not: the RUNNER differs, the spawned binary
// under test (`maxon-shv2`) does not. That is deliberate. The subject is shv2's `test` command; the
// bootstrap is here as a second opinion about whether these tests ran at all.
//
// ⭐⭐ **THE OUTCOME CENSUS, AND THE THREE OUTCOMES THIS CORPUS DELIBERATELY DOES NOT PIN.**
// `UnitTestOutcome` names six ways a test can finish, five of them ways to fail, and a corpus that
// pinned only some of them would look exactly like one that pinned them all. Four are pinned here:
// PASS (`all-pass`, `two-files`), FAIL by a failed assertion (`assertion-failure`), FAIL by an error
// let out of the body (`uncaught-throw`), and CRASHED together with the re-run behind it
// (`panic-crashes`). The other three are ABSENT ON PURPOSE, each for its own reason, written down so
// that a reader finding the hole knows it was considered rather than missed:
//
// **TIMED OUT — excluded because the verdict is about the MACHINE, not the compiler.** The deadline
// is wall time, so a case pinning it goes green on an idle box and red on a loaded one. A gate that
// reports which other processes were running is worse than a missing gate.
//
// **DID NOT RUN — excluded because only half of it is constructible.** It needs a process that dies
// before reaching a test AND a re-run that makes no progress either. `panic-crashes` builds the first
// half; nothing in ordinary source produces the second half deterministically, and its `✓` there is
// exactly the evidence that the runner cures the first half rather than reporting it.
//
// ⚠ **LEAKED — excluded because a leak is NOT CONSTRUCTIBLE FROM ORDINARY MAXON. MEASURED
// 2026-09-02, four routes compiled or refused, one read:** a managed global initializer is refused
// (E2045, not a constant expression); a reference cycle is refused outright (E4014, `recursive type
// references are not allowed`); a top-level managed container held to process exit exits **0**; and a
// `Promise` pushed into an array and never awaited exits **0**. The fifth route is shut by
// declaration rather than by measurement: `__Internals.*` is the only raw allocation the tree has,
// and `stdlib/Internals.maxon`'s own header states that the lowering pass rejects a call to it from
// any other source file. That is the
// language's own design rather than a gap in this corpus — `specs/leak-checker.md` states it
// outright, *"leaks indicate a compiler bug rather than a user error"* — so the ONLY fixture that
// could pin LEAKED would be one built on a LIVE COMPILER DEFECT. Such a gate goes green the day the
// defect is fixed and takes the fixture red with it, which is a gate premised on a bug. A stated gap
// beats one that expires silently.
//
// ⛔⛔ **THE WORKING DIRECTORY IS A CONTRACT, AND IT IS THE CHECKOUT ROOT.** A test binary INHERITS
// the working directory of the `maxon test` process that launched it — `TestExecutor.launchTests` passes
// `inheritedWorkingDir()`, and the command never changes directory. So every path in this project is
// written relative to the checkout root, and a run started anywhere else fails LOUDLY in `readableDir`
// rather than quietly finding nothing. That is the same contract, for the same reason, that
// `SpecTestRunner.maxon:44-66` states for the spec harness.
//
// ⛔⛔ **ONE SPAWNING `test` PER FILE, AND THAT IS STRUCTURAL RATHER THAN TIDINESS.** A test FILE is
// the unit one process runs (`TestExecutor.shardsOf` puts a whole file in one launch), and that process
// has a deadline — 5000 ms by default. Every test here spawns a COMPILER, at roughly half a second each,
// so a file holding all of them exceeds the deadline: MEASURED 2026-09-02, twelve of these in one file
// were killed at 5 s under `bin/maxon` and reported a spurious TIMED OUT, while `--timeout=60000` passed
// all twelve. Splitting by case makes each process hold one spawn, which no deadline can catch — and
// unlike a budget written in a comment, it cannot be exceeded by someone adding a case.
//
// ⚠ **THIS PROJECT IS ITS OWN DIRECTORY ON PURPOSE.** `maxon test` compiles every `.maxon` under the
// directory it is GIVEN, so a project holding anything else would compile that too; this one holds
// nothing but these files. An ordinary `.maxon` sibling here is compiled into THIS project, where its
// top-level names join the constants above — fold anything the tests need into a `*.test.maxon`, where
// an ordinary declaration is perfectly legal.

// ⭐ **EXACTLY ONE DECLARATION HERE IS `export`ed, AND IT IS THE ONE A CASE FILE NAMES:**
// `checkFixture`. A declaration in a `*.test.maxon` takes the file tier unless it says otherwise, just
// as a `test` itself does, and shv2 REFUSES an export nothing outside the declaring file reads (E3092 /
// E3093) — so the export list is not a matter of taste here, it is checked. The bootstrap runs no such
// check, which makes shv2 the stricter of the two oracles on exactly this point.

// Where the fixture projects live, relative to the checkout root — see the working-directory contract
// above. This file sits beside that directory, but a path relative to THIS file is not something a
// running binary has, so it is spelled from the root like every other path here.
let FixturesDir = "tests/test-fixtures"

// The binary under test, relative to the checkout root, without its host suffix.
let TestedCompilerStem = "maxon-shv2/.maxon/maxon-shv2"

// The command being exercised.
export let TestedCommandName = "test"

// The two flags EVERY case is run with, here rather than in each fixture's `argv.txt` so they cannot
// drift case by case. Both exist to make stdout byte-reproducible: `--no-timing` drops the per-test
// durations and the `compile …ms, run …ms` trailer, `--color=never` drops the escape sequences a
// terminal would otherwise get. A fixture needing either one turned back off could not be pinned at all.
let ReproducibleTimingFlag = "--no-timing"
let ReproducibleColourFlag = "--color=never"

// The three files a case directory keeps beside its sources. The first two are REQUIRED — a case that
// says nothing about what it expects proves nothing — and `argv.txt` is optional, absent meaning the
// case takes no arguments beyond the two flags above.
let ExpectedStdoutFileName = "expected.txt"
let ExpectedExitFileName = "expected-exit.txt"
let ExtraArgvFileName = "argv.txt"

// This project's own directory, and the one file in it that declares the shared half rather than a case.
// Together they are how the coverage guard counts case files WITHOUT a hand-maintained number: one
// `*.test.maxon` here is this file, and every other one is a case.
let ProjectDir = "tests/test-command"
let SharedHelperFileName = "fixtures.test.maxon"

// The suffix that marks a source file as test code. Both compilers' directory walks drop these, which is
// the whole reason the fixture corpus can sit inside `maxon-shv2/` at all.
let TestSourceSuffix = ".test.maxon"
let MaxonSourceSuffix = ".maxon"

// The per-project build-products directory. The corpus walk below refuses to descend into one: `maxon
// test` stages a COPY of each fixture under `<case>/.maxon/test/staged/` and writes a generated entry
// beside it, so a walk that went in would find this compiler's own generated `.maxon` and report it as a
// fixture breaking the corpus rule. It is skipped for the reason the compiler's own walk skips it — the
// bytes under it are products, not sources.
let ProjectProductsDirName = ".maxon"

// The fixture whose subject is a COMPILE ERROR rather than a test result.

// How many case directories one corpus walk found. Named because it is the number the coverage guard
// reads, not a loop counter.
typealias FixtureCaseTally = int(0 to i64.max)

// An exit code as a fixture states it. It meets the run's own `ExitInt` only inside an assertion, where
// both are cast to the alias `Expect` compares.
typealias RunExitCode = int(i64.min to i64.max)

// A list of paths one directory walk produced. Declared HERE rather than reached for: stdlib's own
// `FilePathArray` is a member of `Directory` and has no file-scope spelling, so it resolves only inside
// programs that export one of their own.
typealias FixturePathArray = Array with FilePath

// ============================================================================================
// THE CORPUS ITSELF
// ============================================================================================

// ⭐⭐ **A CASE NOBODY DECLARED A `test` FOR READS EXACTLY LIKE A CASE THAT PASSED.** That is the one
// thing a file-per-case shape structurally cannot see for itself — the missing file declares nothing,
// so there is nothing to go red — which is why it is asked once, here, of the corpus as a whole.
test 'the fixture corpus is covered case for case'
	// ⚠ **BOTH SIDES ARE BOUND TO LOCALS FIRST, AND THAT IS FORCED BY A COMPILER DIVERGENCE.** A CALL
	// in `Expect.equal`'s `expected:` slot makes the BOOTSTRAP report the overload set as ambiguous
	// (E3007, naming the `i64`, `String` and `i1` overloads); shv2 resolves the identical source without
	// complaint. MEASURED both ways 2026-09-02. Since these tests must compile under BOTH, they take the
	// spelling both accept — which also puts the two values a reader compares on lines of their own.
	let cases = countFixtureCases() as AssertedInt
	let files = countCaseFiles() as AssertedInt
	try Expect.equal(files, expected: cases, message: "the corpus under {FixturesDir} and the case files in {ProjectDir} do not correspond one to one. Each case directory needs a `<case>.test.maxon` beside {SharedHelperFileName}; a case with no test proves nothing and reads exactly like one that passed.")
end 'the fixture corpus is covered case for case'

// ⛔⛔ **EVERY SOURCE IN THE CORPUS MUST BE A `*.test.maxon`, AND NOTHING ELSE ENFORCES IT.**
// `maxon test` compiles every `.maxon` under the project it is given and drops only test sources from
// the RUN ROSTER — so an ordinary `pricing.maxon` beside a fixture's `pricing.test.maxon` is compiled
// into THAT CASE, and a case is exactly what a golden pins. MEASURED 2026-09-02: a spare `probe.maxon`
// under `all-pass` declaring one typealias nothing reads fails the whole case with E3062 and exit 2,
// against a golden pinning exit 0 — so the case reports a defect in `maxon test` that is really a file
// somebody left behind.
//
// ⚠ The usual cure — a `.maxonignore` at the corpus root — CANNOT be used, and that is measured, not
// supposed: with one there the case answers `no .maxon files found`, exit 2 (re-measured 2026-09-02,
// after the corpus moved to `tests/`). The marker excludes its subtree from the very walk under test, so
// it does not protect the fixtures from anything, it deletes the gate. This assertion takes its place,
// and unlike a marker it fires with a sentence saying what to do.
//
// Nothing is lost by folding a fixture's subject into its test file: an ordinary declaration is legal
// there, and only the reverse — a `test` outside a `*.test.maxon` — is refused (E2058).
test 'no corpus directory holds a source that is not a test'
	// ⭐ **BOTH DIRECTORIES, because the header states the rule for both.** Each is a project `maxon test`
	// is pointed at — this one by the acceptance command, each fixture by a case — so an ordinary `.maxon`
	// beside THIS file is compiled into the harness exactly as one beside a fixture is compiled into that
	// case. Asking only about the corpus would have left the rule this project states about itself
	// enforced nowhere.
	var offenders = sweepableSourcesUnder(readableDir(FixturesDir))
	for source in sweepableSourcesUnder(readableDir(ProjectDir)) 'eachHere'
		offenders.push(source)
	end 'eachHere'

	// One STRING and not the array, because `Expect`'s report renders what it was handed: a reader needs
	// the offending paths in `received:`, and a count would tell them only that there were some.
	let sweepable = listedLines(offenders)
	try Expect.isEmpty(sweepable, message: "these files under {FixturesDir} or {ProjectDir} are ORDINARY Maxon sources, which `maxon test` compiles into the project they sit in — failing that case for a reason having nothing to do with the command under test. Fold each declaration into a `*.test.maxon` file; do NOT add a `.maxonignore`, which would hide them from the command this corpus exists to test.")
end 'no corpus directory holds a source that is not a test'

// ⭐⭐ **A COMPILE ERROR NAMES THE FILE THE AUTHOR WROTE, NOT THE COPY THE RUN IS ABOUT TO DELETE.**
//
// `maxon test` compiles a STAGED COPY of the project, so every path the compiler knows is a path under
// `<project>/.maxon/test/staged/` — a directory the author never wrote in, that the next run purges, and
// that this compiler itself marks as not part of the project. MEASURED 2026-09-02, before
// `Compiler.StagedSourceOrigin` existed, this exact fixture reported
// `…/.maxon/test/staged/sneaky.test.maxon:11:10`; the bootstrap named `sneaky.test.maxon:11:10` for the
// identical bytes.
//
// ⛔ **THE FIXTURE CANNOT CATCH THIS AND MUST NOT BE ASKED TO.** Its `expected.txt` compares STDOUT,
// and a diagnostic goes to STDERR — so `reserved-name-refused` passed, byte for byte, throughout. The
// ============================================================================================
// RUNNING ONE CASE
// ============================================================================================

// ⭐⭐ **ONE CASE, BOTH HALVES, REPORTED TOGETHER.** The exit code and the text are genuinely two
// questions — a run can get both wrong at once — and a reader fixing one wants to see the other in the
// same report, so the exit code is asserted first and the text is asserted whatever it said.
//
// ⚠ **THE EXIT CODE IS PINNED SEPARATELY BECAUSE IT IS THE HALF NOTHING ELSE CAN CATCH.** A run that
// prints a perfect report and exits 0 having failed a test is green to every script that reads it, and
// its stdout is byte-identical to the correct run's. `no-tests-found` is the sharpest of these: a
// zero-test run exits 1 on purpose, and a suite that has stopped containing tests reads exactly like one
// where everything passed.
export function checkFixture(caseName String) throws TestFailure
	let caseDir = readableDir("{FixturesDir}/{caseName}")
	let run = runFixture(caseName)

	// ⚠ Bound to locals first for `the fixture corpus is covered case for case`'s reason: a CALL in
	// `Expect.equal`'s `expected:` slot is E3007 on the bootstrap and fine on shv2.
	let expectedExit = expectedExitCodeIn(caseDir, caseName: caseName) as AssertedInt
	let observedExit = run.exitCode() as AssertedInt
	try Expect.equal(observedExit, expected: expectedExit, message: "'{caseName}' exited differently from its {ExpectedExitFileName}. The exit code is the half of this report a SCRIPT reads, and the half that decides whether a red run stops anything. It printed: {run.stdout}")
	let expectedStdout = requiredFixtureText(caseDir, fileName: ExpectedStdoutFileName, caseName: caseName, holds: "the exact stdout this case's run must print")
	let observedStdout = run.stdout
	try Expect.equal(observedStdout, expected: expectedStdout)
end 'checkFixture'

// Spawn this checkout's `maxon-shv2` at one case and collect everything it said.
//
// ⛔ **EVERYTHING THAT WOULD ⛔ THE CASE BEING RUN AT ALL PANICS RATHER THAN FAILING AN ASSERTION.**
// A missing binary, an unspellable path, a spawn that never happened — none of those is the command
// answering wrongly, and a report that made them read alike would send a reader looking for a defect in
// the thing that never ran.
export function runFixture(caseName String) returns CollectedOutput
	let binary = testedCompilerPath()
	if not File.exists(binary) 'noBinary'
		panic("fixtures.test: the gate could not run — there is no compiler at {binary.toString()}. This file's paths are relative to the CHECKOUT ROOT (see the header); run it from there, and build `{TestedCompilerStem}` first.")
	end 'noBinary'

	let caseDir = readableDir("{FixturesDir}/{caseName}")

	return try Subprocess.run(Executable.path(binary), arguments: fixtureArgv(caseDir, caseName: caseName)) otherwise (e) 'spawnFailed'
		panic("fixtures.test: the gate could not run — could not spawn the '{caseName}' case ({e.displayReason()}). It pins what `{TestedCommandName}` prints, and a report nothing compares is a claim.")
	end 'spawnFailed'
end 'runFixture'

// The binary under test, with the suffix this host can actually spawn.
function testedCompilerPath() returns FilePath
	#if os(Windows)
		let spelled = "{TestedCompilerStem}.exe"
	#else
		let spelled = "{TestedCompilerStem}"
	#endif

	return try FilePath.from(spelled) otherwise panic("fixtures.test: the gate could not run — '{TestedCompilerStem}' is not a spellable path")
end 'testedCompilerPath'

// The command line ONE case is run from: the `test` command, the case directory, the two reproducibility
// flags, then whatever the case's own `argv.txt` adds.
function fixtureArgv(caseDir FilePath, caseName String) returns StringArray
	var argv = StringArray.create()
	argv.push(TestedCommandName)
	argv.push(caseDir.toString())
	argv.push(ReproducibleTimingFlag)
	argv.push(ReproducibleColourFlag)

	for extra in extraArgvIn(caseDir, caseName: caseName) 'eachExtra'
		argv.push(extra)
	end 'eachExtra'

	return argv
end 'fixtureArgv'

// A case's own arguments, one per line of its `argv.txt`. OPTIONAL: a case with no such file takes none,
// which is the ordinary shape and not a missing file.
//
// Blank lines are dropped and each argument is trimmed, so a checkout that handed back CRLF cannot turn
// `--list` into `--list\r` — an argument the command would refuse for a reason having nothing to do with
// the case. No argument this corpus can express has meaningful leading or trailing space.
function extraArgvIn(caseDir FilePath, caseName String) returns StringArray
	var extras = StringArray.create()

	let path = caseDir.join("{ExtraArgvFileName}")
	if not File.exists(path) 'takesNoArguments'
		return extras
	end 'takesNoArguments'

	let text = try File.readText(path) otherwise panic("fixtures.test: the gate could not run — the '{caseName}' case has an {ExtraArgvFileName} that could not be read. A case whose arguments cannot be recovered would be run as some OTHER case, silently.")

	for line in text.split("\n") 'eachLine'
		let argument = line.trim()
		if argument.isEmpty() 'blankLine'
			continue
		end 'blankLine'

		extras.push(argument)
	end 'eachLine'

	if extras.isEmpty() 'namesNoArgument'
		panic("fixtures.test: the gate could not run — the '{caseName}' case has an {ExtraArgvFileName} naming no arguments. A case that takes none simply has no such file; an empty one is a fixture half-written, and it would run as though the flag it exists to test were never passed.")
	end 'namesNoArgument'

	return extras
end 'extraArgvIn'

// The exit code a case's run must produce, read from its `expected-exit.txt`.
function expectedExitCodeIn(caseDir FilePath, caseName String) returns RunExitCode
	let text = requiredFixtureText(caseDir, fileName: ExpectedExitFileName, caseName: caseName, holds: "the exit code this case's run must produce, as one integer")
	return try int.fromString(text.trim()) otherwise panic("fixtures.test: the gate could not run — the '{caseName}' case's {ExpectedExitFileName} is not an integer. It must hold exactly the number the run exits with: 0 when every test passed, 1 when one failed or none was found, 2 when the run could not happen at all.")
end 'expectedExitCodeIn'

// One of a case's two REQUIRED expectation files. Its absence is the instrument being broken, never a
// pass: a case directory with no expectation is a fixture somebody started and did not finish, and a
// sweep that skipped it would report exactly what a sweep that checked it and agreed reports.
// `holds` is what the missing file would have said, which is what a reader needs in order to write it.
function requiredFixtureText(caseDir FilePath, fileName String, caseName String, holds String) returns String
	let path = caseDir.join("{fileName}")
	if not File.exists(path) 'noExpectation'
		panic("fixtures.test: the gate could not run — the '{caseName}' case has no {fileName}, so nothing says {holds}. A case directory with no expectation proves nothing, and reads exactly like one that passed.")
	end 'noExpectation'

	return try File.readText(path) otherwise panic("fixtures.test: the gate could not run — the '{caseName}' case's {fileName} exists but could not be read.")
end 'requiredFixtureText'

// ============================================================================================
// WALKING THE CORPUS
// ============================================================================================

// A directory this file's paths name, refused loudly when it is not there. ONE speller, because every
// such refusal is really the same one — the working-directory contract in the header was not met — and
// each site repeating it is how the sentence that explains it comes to exist in only some of them.
function readableDir(spelled String) returns FilePath
	let path = try FilePath.from(spelled) otherwise panic("fixtures.test: the gate could not run — '{spelled}' is not a spellable path")
	if not Directory.isDirectory(path) 'notThere'
		panic("fixtures.test: the gate could not run — there is no directory at '{spelled}'. Every path in this file is relative to the CHECKOUT ROOT, which is the working directory a test binary inherits from `maxon test`; a run started anywhere else finds nothing.")
	end 'notThere'

	return path
end 'readableDir'

// ⭐⭐ **THE TWO HALVES OF THE COVERAGE GUARD, COUNTED FROM THE FILESYSTEM AND NOT FROM A CONSTANT.**
// An earlier version compared the case count against a hand-edited `CoveredFixtureCases`, which is a
// guard only while somebody edits it: adding a fixture AND bumping the number, without adding a case
// file, was green — and the unrun case still "reads exactly like one that passed", which is the very
// thing the guard claims to close. Both sides are now derived, so there is nothing to forget to update.

// How many case directories the corpus holds. Files sitting at the corpus root are not cases and are not
// counted — a stray note beside the directories is not a fixture.
function countFixtureCases() returns FixtureCaseTally
	var cases = 0 as FixtureCaseTally

	for entry in listing(readableDir(FixturesDir)) 'eachEntry'
		if Directory.isDirectory(entry) 'aCase'
			cases = cases + 1
		end 'aCase'
	end 'eachEntry'

	return cases
end 'countFixtureCases'

// How many of this project's files declare a case: every `*.test.maxon` here except the shared half you
// are reading. A file-per-case layout is what makes this countable at all — see the deadline note in the
// header for why the layout is that way.
function countCaseFiles() returns FixtureCaseTally
	var files = 0 as FixtureCaseTally

	for entry in listing(readableDir(ProjectDir)) 'eachEntry'
		let name = entry.filename()
		if name.endsWith(TestSourceSuffix) and name != SharedHelperFileName 'aCaseFile'
			files = files + 1
		end 'aCaseFile'
	end 'eachEntry'

	return files
end 'countCaseFiles'

// Every `.maxon` file under `root` that is NOT a test source, skipping `.maxon/` product directories.
//
// ⚠ **THAT IS A STRICTER RULE THAN THE COMPILER'S WALK, NOT A COPY OF IT.** `Compiler
// .collectMaxonSourcesUnder` also drops the build manifest and prunes on `.maxonignore`; this one does
// neither, so a `build.maxon` here would be reported as an offender it technically is not. That is the
// safe direction and it is deliberate: neither of those two escapes may be used inside this corpus
// anyway — the marker would hide the fixtures from the very command under test (measured; see the
// assertion above) — so a rule that refuses them outright is the rule this project actually wants.
//
// ⚠ The `.maxon/` skip is not an approximation of the marker: `maxon test` stages a copy of each fixture
// there and writes a GENERATED entry source beside it, so a walk that went in would report this
// compiler's own output as a fixture breaking the rule.
function sweepableSourcesUnder(root FilePath) returns StringArray
	var sweepable = StringArray.create()

	for entry in listing(root) 'eachEntry'
		let name = entry.filename()
		if Directory.isDirectory(entry) 'aDirectory'
			if name == ProjectProductsDirName 'buildProducts'
				continue
			end 'buildProducts'

			for nested in sweepableSourcesUnder(entry) 'eachNested'
				sweepable.push(nested)
			end 'eachNested'
			continue
		end 'aDirectory'

		if name.endsWith(MaxonSourceSuffix) and not name.endsWith(TestSourceSuffix) 'anOrdinarySource'
			sweepable.push(entry.toString())
		end 'anOrdinarySource'
	end 'eachEntry'

	return sweepable
end 'sweepableSourcesUnder'

// The paths a refusal lists, one per line — empty when there are none, which is what makes the assertion
// above an `isEmpty` on the very text a reader has to act on.
function listedLines(paths StringArray) returns String
	var listed = ""
	for path in paths 'eachPath'
		listed.append("\n  {path}")
	end 'eachPath'

	return listed
end 'listedLines'

// What one directory holds. A listing that fails is the instrument being broken — the corpus is checked
// into this repository, so a directory under it that cannot be read is not a fact about any fixture.
function listing(dir FilePath) returns FixturePathArray
	return try Directory.list(dir) otherwise panic("fixtures.test: the gate could not run — could not list {dir.toString()}")
end 'listing'
